            "minVersion": "25.0",
            "projectUrl": "https://helpx.adobe.com/content/dam/help/en/premiere-pro/how-to/in-app/sound-design-travel-video/Sound-Design.prproj",
            "sizeBytes": "123456789",
            "steps": [
                {
                    "stepItems": [
                        {
                            "autoplay": true,
                            "type": "video",
                            "videoUrl": "https://helpx.adobe.com/content/dam/help/en/premiere-pro/how-to/in-app/sound-design-travel-video/423de751-b9d9-4c3a-be5d-61f12b67b9b0.mp4"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "Complement your clips with sound design",
                            "type": "heading"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "Adding sound effects and ambience is a great way to add to your video's mood. Use the <$Essential Sound$> panel to add different effects to your video. For consistency, I've opened a new project with a draft sequence that you can practice on, but you can follow these steps using your own project.",
                            "type": "text"
                        },
                        {
                            "listItems": [
                                {
                                    "text": "In the application menu, choose <$Window$> > <$Essential Sound$> to open the panel."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "Switch to the <$Browse$> tab and then select the <$SFX$> option. There are both free and paid options available. To only show free assets, open the <$Filters$> toggle and choose <$Free$>."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "Find a clip in your draft sequence you'd like to add a sound effect to."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "Use the search bar to find ambient sounds that fit the scene you chose. When you find one you like, drag it from the <$Essential Sound$> panel and drop it onto an audio track. Repeat these last two steps until you've added sound effects throughout your video."
                                }
                            ],
                            "type": "listSubsteps"
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "stepItems": [
                        {
                            "autoplay": true,
                            "type": "video",
                            "videoUrl": "https://helpx.adobe.com/content/dam/help/en/premiere-pro/how-to/in-app/sound-design-travel-video/340513a1-6204-4084-94d4-04f34e720857.mp4"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "Mixing your music and sound effects",
                            "type": "heading"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "We've already added a music track to this film, but it needs to be refined a bit before we're done with our sound mix.",
                            "type": "text"
                        },
                        {
                            "texts": [
                                "I recommend working in the <$Audio$> workspace during this step in the editing process. You can switch by choosing <$Window$> > <$Workspaces$> > <$Audio$>."
                            ],
                            "type": "well",
                            "wellType": "beware"
                        },
                        {
                            "texts": [
                                "<$Please note:$> Switching to the <$Audio$> workspace will close the <$Learn$> panel and hide this tutorial. You can reopen it while using the <$Audio$> workspace by choosing <$Window$> <$Learn$>. You can switch back to the <$Learning$> workspace by choosing <$Window$> > <$Workspaces$> > <$Learning$>."
                            ],
                            "type": "well",
                            "wellType": "info"
                        },
                        {
                            "listItems": [
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Timeline$> panel, right-click on track <$Audio1$> and rename it."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the timeline, use the fade handle to add a <$Fade in$> effect to the music clip."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "If you did not switch to the audio workspace, choose <$Window$> > <$Audio track mixer$>."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Audio Track Mixer$> panel, open the <$Show/Hide Effects$> menu. Then, in the Music track, click one of the <$Effect Selection$> arrows and choose <$Amplitude and Compression$> > <$Amplify$>."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "Double-click on the word <$Amplify$> to open the <$Track Fx Editor$> pop-up window. Use the sliders to lower the volume of all the clips on the Music track."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the toolbar, click and hold on the <$Ripple Edit$> tool and choose the <$Remix$> tool. Drag the end of the music track to where you want it to end."
                                }
                            ],
                            "type": "listSubsteps"
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "stepItems": [
                        {
                            "successHeading": "Great job!",
                            "successText": "Your video is really coming together now that you've added music and sound effects. In the next step, you'll learn to use the <$Lumetri Color$> panel to color correct and grade your films.",
                            "type": "endScreen"
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ]
        },
